### The return value

`sscanf` returns the **number of receiving arguments it assigned to**:

- 3 here means all three values were read;
- fewer means it stopped early: the variables after that point were **not** changed (they keep whatever they held, maybe garbage);
- 0 means the very first conversion failed;
- `EOF` (a negative constant from `<stdio.h>`, -1 on our machines) means the string ended (or held only spaces) before the first conversion was even tried.

So the program above tests how many values it may use. In your own code the usual check is simply `if (n != 3) { error }`.

